54
00:04:09,653 --> 00:04:14,455
It runs some, open source or open
weight models like Qwen, Llama,

55
00:04:14,460 --> 00:04:16,896
Mistral, whatever the teams need.

56
00:04:17,507 --> 00:04:20,650
And this is model as a
service in a nutshell.

57
00:04:20,698 --> 00:04:25,249
And if it sounds like database as a
service from ten years ago, that's

58
00:04:25,249 --> 00:04:27,630
because it's kind of the same idea.

59
00:04:28,327 --> 00:04:34,004
So let's figure it out step by step,
the way you would do it after getting

60
00:04:34,016 --> 00:04:39,768
that ticket, and we start from, from
the very bottom, from the basics.

61
00:04:41,011 --> 00:04:42,551
So what is a model?

62
00:04:43,244 --> 00:04:47,346
the LLM goes first in our rounds
and it says, "Before you run

63
00:04:47,346 --> 00:04:48,670
me, understand what I am."

64
00:04:49,272 --> 00:04:54,632
And a model is just files, right?

65
00:04:54,904 --> 00:04:59,900
it sounds like magic, but for a platform
engineer, it's something very boring.

66
00:05:00,016 --> 00:05:00,817
It's files.

67
00:05:01,131 --> 00:05:04,082
You go to Hugging Face, and
model is a folder of files.

68
00:05:04,982 --> 00:05:09,619
And inside of these files are
numbers, a lot of numbers, like

69
00:05:09,626 --> 00:05:16,351
Qwen thirteen B model, where
fourteen B means billion numbers.

70
00:05:17,140 --> 00:05:20,719
They were learned during training,
and after that they never change.

71
00:05:20,745 --> 00:05:23,498
We call them weights or parameters.

72
00:05:24,228 --> 00:05:30,366
And if you run databases, think of
it, as the data files of the database,

73
00:05:30,510 --> 00:05:36,472
big, read only, and, really useless
until something, loads them, right?

74
00:05:37,692 --> 00:05:41,166
So what does the model
do with these numbers?

75
00:05:41,193 --> 00:05:42,823
You give it text.

76
00:05:43,215 --> 00:05:45,197
Kubernetes is…

77
00:05:45,394 --> 00:05:48,048
And the model pr- predicts
the next piece of text.

78
00:05:48,389 --> 00:05:54,072
Here, a, a token in that place,
roughly three quarters of a word,

79
00:05:54,072 --> 00:05:55,593
then it takes everything again.

80
00:05:55,950 --> 00:06:00,928
now Kubernetes is a, and predicts
the next token, container.

81
00:06:01,498 --> 00:06:05,670
And again, the prediction is
happening, orchestrator, one token

82
00:06:05,670 --> 00:06:08,709
at a time until the answer is there.

83
00:06:08,776 --> 00:06:12,603
That's why you see chatbots
type the answers word by word.

84
00:06:13,422 --> 00:06:14,755
and here's the important part.

85
00:06:15,297 --> 00:06:24,043
every single token is math on all 14
billion numbers, well, in a nutshell.

86
00:06:24,514 --> 00:06:28,063
That's a lot of math, and it must be
very fast, and that's why we need GPUs.

87
00:06:29,029 --> 00:06:30,666
And remember two things from this slide.

88
00:06:30,677 --> 00:06:33,533
First, the work is measured
in tokens, in and out.

89
00:06:33,545 --> 00:06:37,022
And the second, the first
token takes the longest.

90
00:06:37,819 --> 00:06:39,307
Both come back later.

91
00:06:40,892 --> 00:06:42,008
so what is…

92
00:06:42,045 --> 00:06:43,782
What does it take to serve a model?

93
00:06:44,116 --> 00:06:47,831
You need the weights, the, the, the
files that we just talked about.

94
00:06:48,479 --> 00:06:52,971
You need the, what's called an
inference engine, the program that

95
00:06:53,203 --> 00:06:55,190
loads the weights and does the math.

96
00:06:55,246 --> 00:07:00,261
we use vLLM as, I think, the most
popular one in cloud native world.

97
00:07:00,974 --> 00:07:05,947
and if the weights are the data
files, vLLM is the database server.

98
00:07:06,387 --> 00:07:10,332
It takes requests, it batches
them together, it manages memory.

99
00:07:10,917 --> 00:07:16,636
Then you need a GPU to run it
on, graphical processing unit.

100
00:07:17,378 --> 00:07:23,889
And what you get as an end result,
you get HTTP endpoint, and, it's

101
00:07:23,900 --> 00:07:26,117
a OpenAI-compatible endpoint.

102
00:07:26,580 --> 00:07:31,272
And, good that vLLM actually speaks
the OpenAI API out of the box,

103
00:07:31,579 --> 00:07:34,750
so any OpenAI client just works.

104
00:07:34,768 --> 00:07:41,007
You don't need to change anything Now, the
one piece of math you need for the whole

105
00:07:41,007 --> 00:07:44,303
talk, all weights must sit in GPU memory.

106
00:07:44,797 --> 00:07:50,502
Each number takes two bytes, so
parameters times two equals gigabytes.

107
00:07:50,699 --> 00:07:55,602
For example, a four billion parameter
model is about eight gigabytes of RAM.

108
00:07:56,302 --> 00:08:02,178
Qwen 3, 14 billion is
30, then 32 is 62 gigs.

109
00:08:02,341 --> 00:08:07,500
Llama, which is 70 billion
parameters, is 141 gigs, and it

110
00:08:07,500 --> 00:08:09,539
doesn't even fit on this slide.

111
00:08:10,416 --> 00:08:20,614
So now the GPUs, a 20 gigabyte card
like we use, which is for, for our test,

112
00:08:20,632 --> 00:08:24,857
which is RTX, 4000, it has just 20 gigs.

113
00:08:25,350 --> 00:08:30,598
And even on 80 gigabyte card,
which is usually H100, it

114
00:08:30,610 --> 00:08:32,575
doesn't fit the 70 billion model.

115
00:08:32,704 --> 00:08:35,376
So keep this rule in your
head, parameters times two.

116
00:08:35,570 --> 00:08:38,013
We'll need it both, on the next slides.

117
00:08:39,705 --> 00:08:44,413
So what fills the GPU?

118
00:08:45,340 --> 00:08:49,572
The model is 70 billion
parameters, okay, or 14 billion

119
00:08:49,572 --> 00:08:52,235
is 30 gigs, why it might not fit?

120
00:08:53,190 --> 00:08:57,134
Well, the, the thing is that weights
are not the only thing in memory.

121
00:08:57,893 --> 00:09:03,731
Here is our 20 gigabyte card with a
small four billion parameters model.

122
00:09:03,995 --> 00:09:10,344
The weights take eight gigabytes, and
the rest goes to the key value cache.

123
00:09:10,417 --> 00:09:13,244
That's the memory of every
conversation that's in progress,

124
00:09:13,244 --> 00:09:14,869
what has been said so far.

125
00:09:15,091 --> 00:09:18,134
Every open request takes a piece of it.

126
00:09:18,475 --> 00:09:24,242
So database people, this is
your buffer pool or work memory.

127
00:09:24,871 --> 00:09:28,967
And, when it's full, new
requests have to wait in a queue.

128
00:09:29,361 --> 00:09:33,667
So the model fits is not the
same as the model serves.

129
00:09:34,059 --> 00:09:37,463
Free memory after the weights decide
how many users you can serve at the

130
00:09:37,465 --> 00:09:45,650
same time, so the queue is also the
number we'll scale on later on So, okay,

131
00:09:45,653 --> 00:09:47,269
let, let, let's get down to business.

132
00:09:47,271 --> 00:09:49,915
Let's, see how easy day one is.

133
00:09:50,307 --> 00:09:55,437
So on a single machine with a GPU, you
just run vllm serve and the model name.

134
00:09:55,552 --> 00:09:58,189
VLLM downloads the weights
and starts a server.

135
00:09:59,037 --> 00:10:03,725
Then you send a normal OpenAI
style request with curl, and you

136
00:10:03,727 --> 00:10:05,208
get an answer, and that's it.

137
00:10:05,266 --> 00:10:08,045
On one machine, an LLM is one command.

138
00:10:08,061 --> 00:10:12,612
Everything after that point is
what it takes to turn this into

139
00:10:12,614 --> 00:10:14,756
a service for a whole company.

140
00:10:16,282 --> 00:10:17,631
So let's bring Kubernetes.

141
00:10:18,484 --> 00:10:22,593
Kubernetes comes in and
says, "Okay, nice command.

142
00:10:23,055 --> 00:10:25,757
Now try and run it in my cluster."

143
00:10:26,618 --> 00:10:26,956
Right?

144
00:10:26,989 --> 00:10:31,788
And the naive way, the first
thing that anyone does is, okay,

145
00:10:31,788 --> 00:10:33,464
I'm gonna have just a deployment.

146
00:10:33,986 --> 00:10:38,464
The image is vllm container.

147
00:10:38,490 --> 00:10:45,663
The arguments are the model name, and
one important line, nvidia.com/gpu1,

148
00:10:45,706 --> 00:10:51,639
which means that we're going to use, GPU,
in, that are available i- in the cluster.

149
00:10:51,665 --> 00:10:56,662
And the scheduler then treats
them, these workloads like, regular

150
00:10:56,676 --> 00:11:02,669
containers, but it searches for GPU
where to put it Okay, so you have it.

151
00:11:02,691 --> 00:11:04,066
You have one GPU.

152
00:11:04,155 --> 00:11:09,459
Now this pod lands on this GPU and
nobody else does, so, that's it.

153
00:11:10,098 --> 00:11:13,010
Kubernetes finds a node with
a free GPU and starts a pod

154
00:11:13,011 --> 00:11:15,401
with vLLM, and yeah, it works.

155
00:11:15,431 --> 00:11:20,649
Honestly, that's, this is fine first
day for every platform developer.

156
00:11:20,921 --> 00:11:23,042
Okay, the experiment, succeeded.

157
00:11:23,904 --> 00:11:28,387
Now, every platform
developer goes into day two.

158
00:11:29,317 --> 00:11:33,917
So pod restarts and it
downloads the whole model again.

159
00:11:34,363 --> 00:11:38,216
It takes, I don't know, sometimes
15 minutes, sometimes an hour,

160
00:11:38,234 --> 00:11:39,570
depending on the model size.

161
00:11:40,758 --> 00:11:44,751
A new version of the model comes
out and you roll it out by hand.

162
00:11:45,216 --> 00:11:48,391
You need to change all your YAML
manifests and roll them out.

163
00:11:49,382 --> 00:11:54,454
every model needs its own flags, right?

164
00:11:54,694 --> 00:11:57,824
For example, memory,
context length, parallelism.

165
00:11:58,095 --> 00:12:01,303
You keep them in your head or
you calculate them somehow,

166
00:12:01,358 --> 00:12:02,742
again, putting them in YAML.

167
00:12:04,256 --> 00:12:09,380
The next team comes in and copies
your YAML, changes three lines, and

168
00:12:09,380 --> 00:12:12,019
now there are 10 copies to maintain.

169
00:12:12,075 --> 00:12:17,561
Well, of course, if you have GitOps
or some, Git framework or source code,

170
00:12:17,670 --> 00:12:19,283
then you don't have this problem.

171
00:12:20,120 --> 00:12:23,956
So overall, this sounds kind of
familiar to me at least, 'cause I

172
00:12:23,964 --> 00:12:29,118
was working on Database as a Service
for quite some time, and this was

173
00:12:29,120 --> 00:12:31,060
the problem like five, 10 years ago.

174
00:12:31,106 --> 00:12:36,088
We all ran Postgres as a StatefulSet
with a pile of YAML, and the answer was

175
00:12:36,201 --> 00:12:43,176
operators, software that knows how to
run the thing for you And, that's exactly

176
00:12:43,176 --> 00:12:46,735
what's happening t- in today world of AI.

177
00:12:47,107 --> 00:12:50,917
For models, the operator
that's out there is KServe.

178
00:12:50,963 --> 00:12:56,546
It's a popular open source project for
running AI inference on Kubernetes.

179
00:12:56,922 --> 00:13:00,564
You write one object, an
LLM inference service.

180
00:13:00,981 --> 00:13:03,192
This one is simplified on the screen.

181
00:13:03,581 --> 00:13:09,545
Which model, how many GPUs, how many
replicas, and KServe is doing what

182
00:13:09,700 --> 00:13:12,470
every operator does, is just reconciles.

183
00:13:12,499 --> 00:13:15,266
It watches that objects and
keeps the world in line with it.

184
00:13:15,908 --> 00:13:21,910
And the world is a pod with
vLLM on a GPU, and if the pod

185
00:13:21,947 --> 00:13:23,840
dies, KServe brings it back.

186
00:13:23,852 --> 00:13:26,239
If you change the spec, it rolls it out.

187
00:13:26,470 --> 00:13:30,468
The vLLM flags live in
KServe, not in your, head.

188
00:13:31,562 --> 00:13:38,757
So now Kubernetes got its object, one
model running properly, but one model

189
00:13:38,773 --> 00:13:42,472
for one team is not a platform yet.

190
00:13:44,103 --> 00:13:49,132
So now the platform engineer comes
into the bar, because the word got

191
00:13:49,132 --> 00:13:51,847
out and other teams want models too.

192
00:13:52,959 --> 00:13:58,281
So the first problem that we faced,
or any platform engineer is going to

193
00:13:58,282 --> 00:14:02,491
face, is, each model has its own URL.

194
00:14:02,949 --> 00:14:07,230
Four models, four URLs, and every
app needs to know all of them.

195
00:14:07,933 --> 00:14:09,307
We want one endpoint.

196
00:14:09,832 --> 00:14:11,140
So look at the request.

197
00:14:11,179 --> 00:14:18,000
In the body, there is a field called
model, and here it says Qwen13B

198
00:14:19,427 --> 00:14:22,070
So we put an AI gateway in front.

199
00:14:22,439 --> 00:14:24,675
We use Envoy AI gateway.

200
00:14:24,675 --> 00:14:27,738
It's kind of standard, but there
are a bunch of other solutions.

201
00:14:28,087 --> 00:14:33,957
And the difference between AI gateway
and the normal load balancer is that

202
00:14:33,983 --> 00:14:42,078
AI gateway looks at the URL and,
looks at the, data in this URL and

203
00:14:42,088 --> 00:14:47,607
reads this model field, and then it
routes the request to the right model.

204
00:14:47,729 --> 00:14:48,855
Llama doesn't see it.

205
00:14:49,345 --> 00:14:54,883
It's not the only jro- job of AI
gateway, but it's kinda the basic

206
00:14:54,904 --> 00:14:57,246
thing that any AI gateway should do.

207
00:14:57,256 --> 00:15:00,073
Read this model field and
route it to the correct model.

208
00:15:00,540 --> 00:15:04,106
And for the client, nothing
is custom, nothing is changes.

209
00:15:04,110 --> 00:15:06,455
It's OpenAI SDK, right?

210
00:15:06,489 --> 00:15:13,094
You only change the base URL, but
inside you send the same requests.

211
00:15:13,461 --> 00:15:19,984
For the world of databases, it's like a
proxy, like a pgbouncer or ProxySQL, so

212
00:15:19,985 --> 00:15:25,317
that you don't need to figure out where is
your, secondary or primary, for example.

213
00:15:25,621 --> 00:15:30,770
Or if you're sharding databases, you don't
need to figure out where your data is.

214
00:15:31,119 --> 00:15:36,326
Your, sharding proxy just routes it
to the exact, node where the data

215
00:15:36,331 --> 00:15:45,893
is another interesting thing that we
faced is, the question, who is calling?

216
00:15:47,008 --> 00:15:56,198
And the easy start is one API key
for the model, and every app uses it.

217
00:15:56,214 --> 00:16:00,360
So you deploy the model through
your platform, and you have some API

218
00:16:00,368 --> 00:16:05,175
key, you give it out to users, and
then everyone is using this API key.

219
00:16:05,370 --> 00:16:11,034
That's a shared admin password in the
world of databases, and if it leaks in

220
00:16:11,034 --> 00:16:15,732
the Slack channel, you need to rotate it
and break everybody flows, and they need

221
00:16:15,732 --> 00:16:17,762
to rotate it in their apps, and so on.

222
00:16:17,767 --> 00:16:24,728
So that's not the good way to go, and,
the usual approach is that either every

223
00:16:24,730 --> 00:16:28,028
user or every app gets its own key.

224
00:16:28,029 --> 00:16:29,201
Same as databases.

225
00:16:29,231 --> 00:16:33,167
You, you don't give every
app the Postgres superuser.

226
00:16:33,175 --> 00:16:35,270
You create a user per application.

227
00:16:35,648 --> 00:16:40,619
And each key lives in its own file,
rotate one, and nobody else notice it.

228
00:16:40,906 --> 00:16:44,131
Delete it, and only that app loses access.

229
00:16:44,516 --> 00:16:46,242
So that's how it should be done.

230
00:16:46,253 --> 00:16:50,260
That's how we did it in the platform,
even not from the first iteration.

231
00:16:51,633 --> 00:16:55,478
Another important thing
for the platform is limits.

232
00:16:56,094 --> 00:17:01,784
So remember that the work for,
LLMs is measured in tokens.

233
00:17:02,261 --> 00:17:07,930
So one request can be ten tokens or
ten thousand, so counting requests

234
00:17:07,945 --> 00:17:11,132
doesn't work, so we count tokens.

235
00:17:11,172 --> 00:17:16,209
And here, each key gets three
thousand tokens, per hour.

236
00:17:16,917 --> 00:17:19,496
And the chat app used eighteen hundred.

237
00:17:19,602 --> 00:17:20,361
It's fine.

238
00:17:20,454 --> 00:17:22,778
The batch job used all three thousand.

239
00:17:22,934 --> 00:17:26,035
So what's going to happen with the
batch job when it tries to send

240
00:17:26,050 --> 00:17:31,830
requests to a vLLM, or through
to, through OpenAI endpoint?

241
00:17:31,838 --> 00:17:34,242
It gets too many requests error.

242
00:17:34,740 --> 00:17:39,142
And the chat apps continues
working 'cause, it still has a

243
00:17:39,143 --> 00:17:41,374
lot of tokens, in the bucket.

244
00:17:42,308 --> 00:17:48,053
And the way we do it in the platform
is that we are also looking at the

245
00:17:48,053 --> 00:17:56,067
tokens on AI Gateway, and the gateway
keeps the counters in Redis or, Valkey.

246
00:17:56,086 --> 00:17:59,971
We, we have quite a big blog
post about it, how to build this

247
00:18:00,058 --> 00:18:02,273
architecture with Open Everest.

248
00:18:02,672 --> 00:18:04,047
you can find it later.

249
00:18:05,608 --> 00:18:06,081
Okay.

250
00:18:06,351 --> 00:18:11,381
So- Now let's see what
other problems do we have.

251
00:18:11,702 --> 00:18:18,041
And, that is the kind of usual, remember
I was talking about it, that models are

252
00:18:18,042 --> 00:18:24,812
quite big, and, it's fine when you have
just a small model that fits a single GPU.

253
00:18:25,255 --> 00:18:30,487
But now a team wants a big one because
they need to run some big experiment.

254
00:18:31,009 --> 00:18:41,505
So let's say Qwen 3 14 billion parameters,
it's not gonna fit our 20 GB card that

255
00:18:41,530 --> 00:18:44,116
we are running, for our experiments.

256
00:18:44,772 --> 00:18:51,152
So vLLM starts, tries to load the
weights, and it dies with out of memory.

257
00:18:51,654 --> 00:18:55,198
No amount of YAML fixes physics, right?

258
00:18:55,855 --> 00:19:02,763
What you can do is you can buy bigger
GPUs, or we can leverage the beauty of

259
00:19:02,773 --> 00:19:09,835
Kubernetes that allows you to scale things
horizontally and, What you need to know

260
00:19:09,860 --> 00:19:14,793
is that model, remember, it's files.

261
00:19:14,910 --> 00:19:20,360
But if you look a bit on a higher
level, a model is a stack of layers.

262
00:19:20,969 --> 00:19:32,196
So Qwen 30, 340B has 40 layers,
and a token goes through layer

263
00:19:32,197 --> 00:19:35,235
one, then two, and then to 40.

264
00:19:36,541 --> 00:19:46,042
So what we can do, so we can put
these layers on different nodes.

265
00:19:46,462 --> 00:19:51,832
So each GPU is going to hold about ten
gigabytes of weights, and the rest of

266
00:19:51,832 --> 00:19:54,681
its memory goes to the key value cache.

267
00:19:55,523 --> 00:20:00,192
and the way it works is that
one pod is called the head.

268
00:20:00,726 --> 00:20:02,367
It serves the API.

269
00:20:02,880 --> 00:20:05,310
The other two are workers.

270
00:20:05,356 --> 00:20:08,366
A request goes through
all three, in order.

271
00:20:09,194 --> 00:20:13,116
And the way it's called
is pipeline parallelism.

272
00:20:13,809 --> 00:20:19,237
For database people, again, it's a bit
like sharding, except every request

273
00:20:19,258 --> 00:20:22,579
visits every shard one after another.

274
00:20:22,981 --> 00:20:28,759
VLLM does the math, and KServe runs
the group of pods with Kubernetes

275
00:20:28,788 --> 00:20:31,001
project called Leader Worker Set.

276
00:20:31,684 --> 00:20:33,807
And for the client, nothing changes.

277
00:20:33,815 --> 00:20:36,464
Same URL, same model, same key.

278
00:20:36,882 --> 00:20:41,500
One catch, though, that these
three pods are one model, so

279
00:20:41,500 --> 00:20:43,431
they live and die together.

280
00:20:43,730 --> 00:20:47,263
If one worker restarts,
the whole group restarts.

281
00:20:47,542 --> 00:20:53,040
And replicas, two doesn't add one
GPU, it adds a second group of three.

282
00:20:53,230 --> 00:20:59,085
So you would end up with, six pods,
so you need actually six, GPUs.

283
00:20:59,450 --> 00:21:05,315
But i-if you need resiliency,
that's the way to go Okay.

284
00:21:05,589 --> 00:21:12,438
So looks like we solved the
problem with, running it at scale.

285
00:21:12,443 --> 00:21:14,090
We solved the problem with keys.

286
00:21:14,092 --> 00:21:16,465
We solved the problem with scalability.

287
00:21:16,979 --> 00:21:24,224
so the next problem is who is
paying for all of this, right?

288
00:21:24,987 --> 00:21:28,820
And finance team asks
a different question.

289
00:21:29,784 --> 00:21:33,641
Like, okay, so looks like
we bought a lot of GPUs.

290
00:21:34,341 --> 00:21:35,813
What are we doing with those?

291
00:21:35,854 --> 00:21:37,187
Who is using it, and so on.

292
00:21:37,190 --> 00:21:40,528
So there is a lot of questions from
finance, from legal, from other

293
00:21:40,528 --> 00:21:42,177
teams, from other stakeholders.

294
00:21:42,805 --> 00:21:47,830
So we need to answer these questions,
in two different places actually.

295
00:21:48,523 --> 00:21:53,003
So first one, is the
model actually healthy?

296
00:21:53,585 --> 00:21:58,812
so that's actually the question of Q&A
is, like, is it really working, right?

297
00:21:59,379 --> 00:22:03,692
So the metrics are coming from vLLM.

298
00:22:04,227 --> 00:22:07,449
the two numbers I look at
first, time to first token.

299
00:22:08,106 --> 00:22:11,807
Remember that the first token
is the slow one, and it's what

300
00:22:11,964 --> 00:22:15,467
users feel, and requests waiting.

301
00:22:15,638 --> 00:22:18,694
That's the queue for their
key value cache slide.

302
00:22:18,714 --> 00:22:24,859
So remember, it's queuing up and it
slides to the left, so it's going out.

303
00:22:25,397 --> 00:22:31,244
And if it grows, it means that GPU is
full And then the second question is

304
00:22:31,244 --> 00:22:33,572
who is using it or who is paying for it?

305
00:22:34,101 --> 00:22:35,566
that comes from the gateway.

306
00:22:35,592 --> 00:22:42,041
So we said that we count tokens per
key, and we store it somewhere in

307
00:22:42,041 --> 00:22:44,724
Redis or Valkey or tokens per model.

308
00:22:45,111 --> 00:22:51,062
So both in-- go into the same
Prometheus or Grafana dashboard, so

309
00:22:51,062 --> 00:22:54,173
you can send all the metrics to those.

310
00:22:54,521 --> 00:22:59,624
And, this is where one key per app
actually pays off, 'cause every week

311
00:22:59,625 --> 00:23:02,454
someone asks who used all the GPU.

312
00:23:02,492 --> 00:23:06,833
Because every token at the gateway
is tagged with a key, the answer

313
00:23:06,835 --> 00:23:12,606
is one Prometheus query that,
summarizes, tokens by key owner.

314
00:23:13,330 --> 00:23:17,271
Now you can have that conversation
with data and show it to finance that,

315
00:23:17,272 --> 00:23:22,968
"Hey, Bill from, your team actually
used all the tokens," and the here

316
00:23:22,968 --> 00:23:25,138
was when, show the graph, and so on.

317
00:23:25,352 --> 00:23:31,607
So what I'm saying here is that, the basic
questions like, is it really working?

318
00:23:31,607 --> 00:23:33,557
So ease of availability is there.

319
00:23:34,272 --> 00:23:40,119
so for Kubernetes, for KServe,
everything is coming quite nicely 'cause

320
00:23:40,126 --> 00:23:41,732
you send the metrics to Prometheus.

321
00:23:41,734 --> 00:23:45,556
You can configure your, alert manager
to send the alerts, and so on.

322
00:23:45,951 --> 00:23:51,746
And who is paying is, again, the metrics
that you can store anywhere, again,

323
00:23:51,747 --> 00:23:55,671
in Prometheus, or you can send them
as logs somewhere into ClickHouse,

324
00:23:55,682 --> 00:24:00,182
for example, and then provide some
sophisticated, billing results.

325
00:24:01,164 --> 00:24:06,668
but one important thing that we also
faced, we were playing with, automated

326
00:24:06,677 --> 00:24:13,556
scaling, so to scale, VLLM and, the
model horizontally, automatically.

327
00:24:14,171 --> 00:24:20,549
And usually when you do this
in Kubernetes, you look at CPU.

328
00:24:20,690 --> 00:24:26,349
And just remember, maybe it's obvious, but
maybe it's not super obvious for everyone,

329
00:24:26,815 --> 00:24:29,401
that CPU is usually idle for models.

330
00:24:29,402 --> 00:24:33,174
What you need to look at is,
GPU, and you need to use GPU,

331
00:24:33,461 --> 00:24:36,175
utilization to scale, right?

332
00:24:36,338 --> 00:24:41,350
In KServe, this is called a
workload variant autoscaler.

333
00:24:41,747 --> 00:24:46,800
You set minimum and maximum replicas,
and it adds replicas when the, your

334
00:24:46,986 --> 00:24:51,717
key value queue grows, and that's what
we use in, OpenEverest to scale as

335
00:24:51,717 --> 00:24:58,253
well but remember that scaling up an
LLM is not like scaling up a web app.

336
00:24:58,530 --> 00:25:03,092
traffic spike comes in and,
the new pod gets scheduled.

337
00:25:03,099 --> 00:25:06,980
It pulls the vLLM, image, which
is already several gigabytes.

338
00:25:06,989 --> 00:25:11,801
Then it downloads thirty gigabytes of
weights, then it loads them into the GPU.

339
00:25:12,241 --> 00:25:16,215
So that's actually not
seconds, it's, minutes of time.

340
00:25:16,463 --> 00:25:20,559
And by the time the replica is
ready, the spike might be over.

341
00:25:20,797 --> 00:25:22,384
So database people know this.

342
00:25:22,393 --> 00:25:26,184
It's like adding a replica that
has to restore a big backup first.

343
00:25:26,708 --> 00:25:32,756
So keep the weights close on a volume
in a cluster instead of Hugging Face.

344
00:25:33,150 --> 00:25:38,382
There is also capability to cache
models and, keep at least one replica

345
00:25:38,395 --> 00:25:40,888
running if you have enough resources.

346
00:25:41,944 --> 00:25:43,682
So let's look at what we just built.

347
00:25:43,760 --> 00:25:44,979
Let's step back a bit.

348
00:25:45,840 --> 00:25:50,374
so apps call one AI gateway.

349
00:25:50,598 --> 00:25:54,948
The gateway checks the keys, counts
tokens, and routes by model name.

350
00:25:55,273 --> 00:26:01,185
Then we have KServe that runs the
models, vLLM on GPU, sometimes one

351
00:26:01,187 --> 00:26:03,346
pod, sometimes, a group of three.

352
00:26:03,869 --> 00:26:08,733
And, under all of that, we have proper
metrics, dashboards, and auto-scaling.

353
00:26:09,112 --> 00:26:12,202
And that's a lot of moving
parts, and you need all of it

354
00:26:12,295 --> 00:26:15,182
for every team and every model.

355
00:26:15,191 --> 00:26:17,310
That's what the platform looks like.

356
00:26:17,942 --> 00:26:20,744
And, we've actually built this before.

357
00:26:20,745 --> 00:26:23,603
I mean, we collectively
in engineering world.

358
00:26:24,145 --> 00:26:28,957
so if you run databases platform, you
already know all these primitives.

359
00:26:28,966 --> 00:26:32,356
So for weights, it's data files.

360
00:26:32,368 --> 00:26:35,314
For vLLM, it's the database server.

361
00:26:35,324 --> 00:26:37,259
KServe is the operator.

362
00:26:37,347 --> 00:26:39,076
AI gateway is the proxy.

363
00:26:39,110 --> 00:26:40,933
API key, database user.

364
00:26:41,313 --> 00:26:42,430
what else do we have there?

365
00:26:42,433 --> 00:26:45,293
Splitting across GPUs is sharding.

366
00:26:45,703 --> 00:26:49,896
So different words, but
the same platform problems.

367
00:26:50,559 --> 00:26:55,324
And, For me, as a maintainer of
OpenEverest, in OpenEverest we

368
00:26:55,324 --> 00:26:59,137
have a notion called instance,
and everything is an instance.

369
00:26:59,159 --> 00:27:03,207
Postgres cluster is an instance,
Mongo cluster, Milvus, ClickHouse,

370
00:27:03,220 --> 00:27:06,482
whatever databases we support
there, everything is an instance.

371
00:27:07,053 --> 00:27:13,746
So we actually made a model an instance
too, and, the KServe is plugged

372
00:27:13,747 --> 00:27:17,672
in into OpenEverest as a provider,
we call it providers there, and it

373
00:27:17,672 --> 00:27:22,469
does everything from, that I was
picking in this talk under the hood.

374
00:27:22,556 --> 00:27:28,906
So KServe objects, gateway routes,
keys, token limits, splitting across

375
00:27:28,907 --> 00:27:33,780
nodes, monitoring, everything is in
a single platform, through the same

376
00:27:33,785 --> 00:27:38,289
UI, same API that we use for all
the databases that we have in there.

377
00:27:38,606 --> 00:27:43,390
You can use, YAML manifests or
GitOps approach with creating the

378
00:27:43,422 --> 00:27:45,277
custom resources in Kubernetes.

379
00:27:45,834 --> 00:27:53,625
And, that's the reproducible environment
that platform engineer, is asking for.

380
00:27:54,232 --> 00:27:57,284
So let's go back to the BAR.

381
00:27:57,307 --> 00:28:03,587
So the platform engineer
asked for reproducibility.

382
00:28:04,070 --> 00:28:08,855
So models are instances like databases,
managed like databases with the

383
00:28:08,855 --> 00:28:11,688
same UI, API, and access control.

384
00:28:12,200 --> 00:28:16,821
as many GPUs for an LLM, so
even when it doesn't fit on

385
00:28:16,823 --> 00:28:19,162
one, we split it across nodes.

386
00:28:19,629 --> 00:28:24,013
And for Kubernetes, everything is
an object, and it reconciles with

387
00:28:24,032 --> 00:28:28,799
KServe and other resources under
the hood, AI gateways, and so on.

388
00:28:28,811 --> 00:28:31,725
So everything works as expected.

389
00:28:33,528 --> 00:28:38,089
So as a final thing, if you want
to try it out, it's four steps.

390
00:28:38,089 --> 00:28:44,539
You install OpenEverest on your Kubernetes
cluster, you add the KServe provider,

391
00:28:44,854 --> 00:28:51,035
and it installs KServe itself, then
the Envoy gateway, Leader Worker Set.

392
00:28:51,443 --> 00:28:57,386
Then you deploy model and then
create a key for each application

393
00:28:57,386 --> 00:28:58,541
if you need it, of course.

394
00:28:59,119 --> 00:29:03,637
And everything is open source,
and, we have a step-by-step guide

395
00:29:03,639 --> 00:29:08,922
in our blog, and you can read
all the blogs on OpenEverest.io

396
00:29:08,935 --> 00:29:12,327
or get more information
about OpenEverest there.

397
00:29:12,327 --> 00:29:15,315
It's an open source CNCF product.

398
00:29:16,088 --> 00:29:22,068
So if you try it and something breaks,
I want to hear about it and, you can

399
00:29:22,068 --> 00:29:29,873
find me in CNCF Slack channel or follow
me on LinkedIn and we can talk there.

400
00:29:30,585 --> 00:29:32,773
Thank you and appreciate your time.
